>[]($block.attrs("author-note"))
> I've tried and failed to write various versions of this post a half dozen times over the
> last week or so. This is finally the one that I think gets at what I'm really feeling.
>
> Honestly I think I'm just having a quarter life crisis really and this is how it's making itself
> known.

[]($section)
## Craft
I care a lot about writing code as a craft. I have an inbuilt need to carefully curate my
commit log (which is a big reason I love [`jj`](https://github.com/jj-vcs/jj)). I hunt down
compiler warnings with minimal mercy. I love to tinker and experiment and really try and
get a good handle on my tooling and my development "flow."

And I still write, generally speaking, pretty mediocre code.

I'm going to make a claim that I feel is true but hedge it a bit: I am a *pretty good*
swift programmer. I'm not a *godlike* swift programmer, but I know my way around the
language pretty well. The reason I consider myself decent at the language is because I
spent the first 4.5 years of my career writing pretty much exclusively swift (with the
occassional interjection of the ever-present staples like bash scripts, Dockerfiles, and
the like). Where I got extra lucky here though is that I was writing backend swift code,
not iOS code, and I was writing it while getting code review from some of the best
engineers I've ever met. 

So why does that matter?

Because I feel like it's caused this weird dichotomous thing where as soon as I moved on
from that role from feeling stagnant (a few months ago now), I wanted to explore all the
other fun shiny things out there, but I don't have the same support structure around me
anymore to let me get to that level of skill so rapidly again. It means that I still have
that same little perfection gremlin on my shoulder at all times, but no more real "mentor"
to look over the other shoulder and guide me along.

[]($section)
## Not a Squire, Not a Lord
So now I'm in a spot in my career that I'm sure is familiar to a lot of people: I have a
few years of experience under my belt. I am definitely no longer a junior engineer. Many
of the "here be dragons" gotchas that would have caught me off guard a couple years ago
can now be avoided without a second thought, both from a high level design perspective and
in the nitty-gritty of slinging code.

But at the same time, I'm also not sure I'd call myself "senior." That title is also somewhat
nebulous from place to place, but I use it here in the sense of "could reasonably mentor a
handful of juniors and lead a small project." Do I think that I could do those things
given the opportunity? Yeah. Do I think I would do it well? Not a fucking chance. It feels
like there's still so many things that I'm weak at when it comes to software:
* My low-level understanding is still pretty middling. Sure, I know how to figure out
  what's causing a memory leak, I've written a smattering of assembly, etc, 
  but a lot of the more "primitive" parts of computing are things that I just haven't had
  to directly work with before in the land of high-level languages.
* I haven't had to architect a really large system yet. Sure, I have all kinds of
  thoughts and opinions on how I *think* I would do it (grug say: complexity *very very*
  bad), but until I actually do the only frame of reference are the systems I've worked
  on, for better or worse, without the whole picture in my head.
* I'm pretty specialized language-wise. I'm a decent swift programmer. I can write at
  least acceptable rust and python. That's about it. I pretty firmly believe that any
  engineer worth their salt (hopefully myself included) can learn a new language to an
  acceptable level within a handful of weeks, but even so.

[]($section)
## The LLM Sized Elephant
There's an elephant in the room here in that my career is, unfortunately, coinciding with
the huge AI bubble we now find ourselves in. Setting my own opinions about the tech aside
(though I think my blog makes them pretty clear), it's now yet another thing I have to
think about when presenting my work during job applications. **I** know that I don't use
AI assistance to write code, but there's no real way for me to prove that to people
without them actually reading my code. And, lets be honest, companies don't actually do
that as part of the interview process, or if they do it's once you're already multiple
rounds in, which is past the hard part of getting your foot in the door.

Look, LLMs are here. They're gonna stick around. I've made my peace with that. I just
really wish that they didn't make me feel like I was getting fucked over because I have a
modicum of care about the code I write.

[]($section)
## Skill Issue
All of this comes back to the fact that I just haven't built the skills. I can sit here
and whine about having too many shiny things to play with, or about how it seems like all
the cool software has already been written and I don't wanna just slap together yet
another CRUD app. Sometimes you just gotta get it out there on the page. But the real
solution here is to just go write some shit. Probably some shit in zig, because that
language looks cool.

So I'm gonna go write some shit in zig. I have no idea what it will be, and it probably
won't be useful to anyone else, but god dammnit if I don't want to rediscover what got me
into this field in the first place.
